 <u> SUMMARY </u> :  Requires high school pupils beginning in 2004 to pass  
a high school exit exam in order to graduate.  Specifically,  
 <u> this bill: </u> 

1)Requires the Superintendent of Public Instruction (SPI), with  
  the approval of the State Board of Education (SBE), to develop  
  a high school exit exam in language arts and mathematics in  
  accordance with SBE adopted academically rigorous content  
  standards.

2)Requires SBE to adopt a high school exit examination that is  
  aligned to state content standards by October 1, 2000.

3)Requires that all students in the graduating class of  
  2003-2004, and all later years, pass the exit examination in  
  order to receive a diploma of high school graduation.

4)Requires SPI to appoint a High School Exit Examination  
  Standards Panel to assist with the development of the test.   
  The panel, among others, must be comprised of teachers,  








                                                         <u> SB 2 X1
                                                         </u> Page  2

  administrators, school board members, and the general public.   
  Members will serve for two years without pay and shall be  
  representative of the state's ethnic, geographical, and  
  cultural diversity and must have gender balance.

5)Requires that the exit examination be field tested prior to  
  implementation to ensure that the examination is free from  
  bias and that its content is valid and reliable.

6)Requires that the exit examination be reviewed by the existing  
  Statewide Pupil Assessment Review (SPAR) Panel to ensure that  
  no questions elicit information concerning personal beliefs or  
  practices.

7)Requires that the high school exit exam:  a) not be  
  administered to a pupil who did not receive adequate notice  
  (as defined) regarding the test; b) not intentionally  
  discriminate against a protected group or class; c) have  
  instructional and curricular validity; and, d) be scored as a  
  criterion referenced exam.  If this is not done, the test  
  shall not be required as a condition of graduation.

8)Provides for accommodation of special education students by  
  requiring the development of differential standards and  
  assessment procedures.  

9)Requires that each pupil takes the exit exam in grade ten  
  starting in 2002 and be allowed to take the exam at each  
  subsequent administration until each part of the exam has been  
  passed.  Pupils are also allowed, but not required, to take  
  the test in grade nine starting in 2001. 

10)Allows districts to defer testing of English Language  
  Learners (ELL) until the pupil has been in California schools  
  for two years, during which time the ELL pupil should have  
  received at least six months of instruction in reading,  
  writing and comprehension in the English language.  Deferring  
  test administration does not exempt the ELL pupil from taking  
  the exit test, and the pupil must still pass the test in  
  English in order to graduate.

11) Requires that supplemental instruction be provided to any  
  pupil who "does not demonstrate sufficient progress toward  
  passing the exit examination."   Specifies that sufficient  
  progress shall be determined on the basis of either the  








                                                         <u> SB 2 X1
                                                         </u> Page  3

  results of STAR and the minimum levels of proficiency  
  specified by the SBE or the pupil's grades and other  
  indicators of academic achievement designated by the school  
  district.

12)Repeals, effective January 1, 2000, existing requirements for  
  establishment of local proficiency standards and assessment of  
  pupils to those standards. 

13)Appropriates $2 million of federal Goals 2000 funding to SPI  
  to develop the high school exit examination.

14)Requires that by January 15, 2000, SPI shall contract for a  
  multiyear independent evaluation of the high school exit  
  examination.  The evaluation shall be based upon information  
  gathered in field testing and annual administrations of the  
  examination and shall include specified information.

15)Provides that after adoption and initial administration of  
  the high school exit examination SBE, in consultation with  
  SPI, shall study the appropriateness of other criteria by  
  which high school pupils who are regarded as highly proficient  
  but unable to pass the high school exit examination may  
  demonstrate their competency and receive a high school  
  diploma.  These criteria may include, but are not limited to,  
  an exemplary academic record as evidenced by transcripts and  
  alternative tests of equal rigor in the academic areas covered  
  by the high school exit examination. If SBE determines that  
  other criteria are appropriate and do not undermine the intent  
  of this chapter that all high school graduates demonstrate  
  satisfactory academic proficiency, SBE shall forward its  
  recommendations to the Legislature for enactment. 

16)Authorizes schools to use regularly available resources and  
  any available supplemental remedial resources to prepare  
  pupils to succeed on the exit exam.

 <u> FISCAL EFFECT</u> :   According to the Assembly Appropriations  
Committee:

1)Appropriates $2 million in federal funds (i.e., Goals 2000)  
  for the development of the high school exit exam.  These funds  
  are provided in the proposed 1999 Budget Act.

2)General Fund (GF)(Non-Proposition 98) costs of $500,000  








                                                         <u> SB 2 X1
                                                         </u> Page  4

  annually to contract out for a multiyear evaluation.

3)GF (Non-Proposition 98) cost of $300,000 annually to SPI to  
  administer the development of the test and the contract for an  
  independent evaluation.

4)GF (Proposition 98) costs of $3-5 million annually to school  
  districts with high schools to offer the test to high school  
  students in the class of 2003-2004, beginning in 2002.

5)GF (Proposition 98) costs of $350,000 annually to school  
  districts for a parental notification, beginning in 2001-2002.  
    This bill states 2003-2004, but should state 2001-2002.
 <u> 
COMMENTS </u> :  This bill is one of four bills sponsored by the  
Governor as part of his call for an extraordinary session of the  
Legislature dedicated to education reform issues.  The other  
three bills address:   accountability [SB 1X (Alpert), pending  
in the Assembly]; peer assistance and review for teachers [AB 1X  
(Villaraigosa), pending in the Senate]; and, reading reform [AB  
2X (Mazzoni), pending in the Senate].


